Date: Fri, 21 May 93 18:31:49 CDT
From: ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul?

An interesting line of argument occurred to me today, so I thought I would
pass it on. One reason AI seems important to many of us is that it provides
a new approach to understanding ourselves--what sort of thing a conscious
being is. I wish to claim that, seen from that standpoint, the history of
AI so far is evidence for the existence of the soul.

To begin with, by "the existence of the soul" I do not mean any particular
religious description of consciousness, proposition about immortality, or
the like. What I mean is the proposition that consciousness as we know it
cannot be explained by straightforward mechanism--that no description of an
assembly of gears and levers, or transistors and circuits, can give an
adequate description of a conscious being. This proposition is, of course,
consistent with a variety of different explanations of what else
consciousness is.

AI is in large part an attempt to describe consciousness as mechanism, and
I presume that most of its practitioners believe that it can be so
described. My claim that AI's history is evidence for the opposite
proposition starts from the idea that a scientific theory should be
falsifiable. If I perform an experiment and claim the outcome is evidence
for X, there must have been an alternative outcome that would have been
evidence against X.

Consider the history of AI as a series of such experiments. It is my
understanding (I claim no expertise in the subject) that proponent of AI
have consistently overestimated what they are going to be able to
accomplish.  If achieving what they said they would would have been
evidence for their views, then failing to achieve it is evidence against.
Put a little differently, predictions of what AI would accomplish were
deduced from a set of assumptions, one of which was that consciousness
could be adequately described as mechanism. The failure of such predictions
is then evidence against all of the assumptions--including that one. Of
course, if you start out very sure of that assumption, you will attribute
almost all of the failure to failure of other assumptions--but unless you
are certain of the assumption, some of the weight of the failure has to
count against it.

Does anyone see a hole in the argument?

David Friedman
University of Chicago Law School


