Aikido is fairly soft and gentle.  The bruises and what have you are
probably because you are still learning techniques like how to fall
properly, correct? Eventually, they will go away. Aikido is a fairly
reactive martial art -- you operate largely on the basis of countering
your opponent. When I used to go to a dojo regularly (my Sensei
simultaneously taught us Hakkoryu Jiu-Jitsu and some form of Karate
that I can't remember -- he was amazingly good, too) at the end of the
evening I usually felt like I'd been beaten up -- largely because for
practical purposes I had. We'd practice blocking over and over and
over again, and that bruises you no matter what. Now, had it just
been Jiu-Jitsu, I suspect I still would have gotten a bit shaken up,
but not nearly as much so. Still, Jiu-Jitsu is fairly agressive --
virtually every kata we learned was peppered with spots where you
could alter your move slightly and break something. I was always a big
fan of wrist locks -- I once very handily avoided a fight with one.

This is not to say that Aikido is not an effective martial art --
virtually all of them are given sufficient mastery. However, there are
different degrees of agression involved -- arts like Tae Kwon Do which
emphasize applying blows to the opponent are markedly more agressive
than arts like Jiu-Jitsu that emphasize catches and throws, and Aikido
goes even further along the spectrum.

Sadly, I haven't taken any martial arts in years and have doubtless
forgotten much of what I know. Its probabhly time to get back into
practice again.

Perry

