  almost all of the failure to failure of other assumptions--but unless you
  are certain of the assumption, some of the weight of the failure has to
  count against it.

If however, you can explain the failure on particular assumptions which
you can demonstrate were poor, you eliminate any aspersion on the
assumption that we can mechanistically produce something with
the functionality of conscious beings.

I have my own nominee for the assumption responsible for failure;
the expert system approach to AI. Concepts were symbolically manipulated, 
but the connection of those concepts to the world was non existent.

What is needed is a system which builds upon fairly elaborate
external sensory mechanisms, an activation mechanism which allows
interaction with the world, and internal sensory mechanisms which
allow monitoring of the activation mechanisms. Then some motivating
mechanism must be built in to get the show running.

In short, the deductive approach to AI needs to be replaced by
an inductive approach. The deductive approach had the
researchers attempting to impose their own conceptual schema
on the AI systems they created. Say what you will about
neural network research, it has given us improvement in certain tasks.
I think it is precisely because the networks were allowed
some latitude in finding their own conceptual representation
of the data that they allowed performance improvement. The more that 
trend continues, the closer AI research will approach human functionality.

(If there are any researchers in artificial life, I'm sure
they would have interesting input on this subject.)

