Date: Fri, 21 May 93 18:04:32 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul?

ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu writes:
>Put a little differently, predictions of what AI would accomplish were
>deduced from a set of assumptions, one of which was that consciousness
>could be adequately described as mechanism. The failure of such predictions
>is then evidence against all of the assumptions--including that one. Of
>course, if you start out very sure of that assumption, you will attribute
>almost all of the failure to failure of other assumptions--but unless you
>are certain of the assumption, some of the weight of the failure has to
>count against it.  Does anyone see a hole in the argument?

The argument is valid, though rather weak.  

First, I'd be more comfortable if the evidence were clearer that
people had mis-predicted the rate of progress.  We'd want to separate
out what folks really believed from the claims of spokesfolk seeking
funding.  And we'd want to check what rate of progress non/anti-AI
folks predicted; maybe they expected too little progress.

Second, the change in belief factor can at most be about the
probability AI folks would have initially assigned that progress would
be this much or less.  I'd doubt if this was less than 1/3.

Third, this same group of people has formed millions of other
expectations which have confronted evidence, such as the prospects for
various kinds of programming languages, environments and
problem-solving techniques, and their rate of progress on many small
grain application areas.  AI folk claim some pretty big computer
advances as results of their work.  The one prediction for overall
progress over the decades should be swamped by all the other
predictions.  One would do better to ask if AI folk predict better or
worse on average than competing schools of thought.

Forth, the AI community is rather diverse, and has changed a lot over
the decades.  I'd say most AI researchers now have no opinion on the
subject of "can consciousness be described by mechanism", and I doubt
if most ever did.  In fact the claim is so fuzzy I'm not sure what I
think about it.

Fifth, the same argument would apply to any other beliefs that this
community shared, like being more liberal than average, liking Chinese
food and MIT, etc.

Robin Hanson, still an AI researcher for the next four months (when
I'll become an economics Ph.D. student at Caltech)

