> understanding (I claim no expertise in the subject) that proponent of AI
> have consistently overestimated what they are going to be able to
> accomplish.  If achieving what they said they would would have been
> evidence for their views, then failing to achieve it is evidence against.

I think the hole in your argument, David, is a confusion of
engineering with science.

Leonardo Da Vinci first hypothesized that flying was possible in a
serious way hundreds of years ago, and men unsuccessfully built flying
machines for centuries before the Wright Brothers did it "right".

Babbage hypothesized that he could build a computer almost a century
early -- his experiment failed, but eventually we succeeded.

The mere fact that something can be built in theory doesn't mean that
we yet have complete knowledge needed to perform the activity.

AI is predicated on the notion that since the human mind is ultimately
based on mechanistic systems (and all scientific evidence points to
this -- note, for instance, the way we can consistantly wipe out rats
memories by extirpating part of their brain and the like -- plenty of
falsifyable experiments there that have succeeded) we should
ultimately be able to build equivalent mechanistic systems ourselves.

You are, however, partially right. The hypothesis that we already had
the capacity and knowledge to build AIs turned out to be wrong.
However, I'd say that its pretty logically evident that if the mind is
an epiphenomenon of a mechanistic system equivalent mechanistic
systems can be built GIVEN SUFFICIENT TECHNOLOGY, just as to Leonardo
the notion that since flight was a phenomenon resulting from a
mechanistic system that he could build equivalent systems GIVEN
SUFFICIENT TECHNOLOGY was a logically sound idea.

When, however, we will achieve this technology is another question.

> Put a little differently, predictions of what AI would accomplish were
> deduced from a set of assumptions, one of which was that consciousness
> could be adequately described as mechanism. The failure of such predictions
> is then evidence against all of the assumptions--including that one.

Ah, but we can perform much more direct experiments which HAVE
succeeded.

Take, for example, a set of people with their brains exposed during
surgery. Electrically stimulate portions of the surface of the brain.
You consistantly get imagery experienced by the subject. Take rats --
cut the right portion of the brain out, and you can achieve,
consistantly, dozens and dozens of effects, from elimination of sex
drive to an inability to remember mazes. Any experiment you can dream
up to ask the question "is the seat of consciousness in the brain, and
does the brain seem to be a mechanistic system" will likely work --
and have.

On the other hand, we have not discovered that AIs can't be built --
we have merely shown that we cannot yet build them.

Perry

