Subject: CHALLENGE: Chaitin and RTFM

Derek Zahn has CHALLENGED me to explain where Chaitin is:

>> clouding the distinction between undecidable and computationally
>> difficult.

in his "Randomness in Arithmetic" Scientific American article, July 1988
Derek then helpfully quotes Chaitin:

> "No matter how bright one is or how long the proofs and how
> complicated the mathematical axioms are, the infinite series of
> propositions stating whether the number of solutions of the
> diophantine equations is finite or infinite will quickly defeat one
> as 'k' increases."

and continues:

> I admit that the wording is unfortunate, but he's not trying to
> say that it's really hard so oh well, but rather that it is impossible
> (not just hard) to characterize the (infinite) series of results with
> finite axiom systems.  Maybe this isn't where you mean that he
> confuses difficult with undecidable.

I could conclude the CHALLENGE by just agreeing that this the text I was
thinking of, but then I would fall short of 400 words, and not be very
helpful to boot.  More helpfully, so that people can see where I'm
coming from, I'll demonstrate that Chaitin _is_ saying that it is 'hard
so oh well' and _not_ that it is impossible.  The easiest way [says he,
after having squirmed for a day over it :-)] is to see that the solving
the expansion os Omega is not impossible, but analogous to the
calculation of expansion of pi, by considering the Busy Beaver numbers
Casti mentions in his Searching for Certainty book that Tim and others
have already quoted from.

The problem all stems from the use of the description 'uncomputable'. 
An uncomputable number is a number that an n-state Turing machine can't
print without risking getting stuck in infinite loop.  n-state refers
to the number of internal states the 'head' can have as it scans over
the input tape of 0s and 1s, overwriting the tape where it wishes.  In
other words it's a measure of the memory of the Turing machine, with
memory/bits = log2 (n).  A finte state Turing machine gets confused when
trying to print a large number (a long series of 1s) because it forgets
how many it has already printed.  For each n-state Turing presented with
a tape of 0s the largest number (a sequence of 1111s) it can print is
called the Busy Beaver number, BB(n), which is a function of n.

The first few values of BB(n) are known:
       n     BB(n)
       1      1
       2      4
       3      6
       4     13
       5      not currently known for sure

thereafter they inflate very rapidly.  According to Casti 
BB(12) >=  6 * (4096^(4096^(4096^(4096^...(4096^4)...)))
where the first ... = a chain of 168 4096^( s.  According to Casti a
number is officially termed 'uncomputable' on a n-state Turing machine
if it exceeds BB(n).  And goes on to say:

     "As we let n become larger and larger, BB(n) + 1 gets closer
     and closer to a truly uncomputable number"   (p446, pb)
                     ^^^^^  (my emphasis)
In other words no number is uncomputable on an infinite state Turing
machine.  No one would find astounding the statement (which I've just
made up):

     My computer can only calculate pi to a certain accuracy,
     determined by its memory and MIPs, before it gives up.

and helps us keep a sense of perspective on things.  Omega is therefore
calculable, just as the expansion of pi is.  Statements describing some
calculation as "absolutely intractable" are seen as predictions about
limits to our computational powers that remain to be empirically
verified.

That is not to deny the existence of Godel-undecidable theorems /
statements / calculations that require an extension of the chosen axiom
base to solve.  But it important, from a metamathematical point of view,
to clearly distinguish between the two concepts.

I hope this has been a helpful response to the CHALLENGE.  In Derek's
reply I would like him to explicitly state whether/where my reasoning
is logically flawed.  If he feels my answer is incomplete (I have skated
over some areas) I will gladly elaborate.

