Date: Sat, 22 May 1993 00:56:58 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul?

David Friedman:
> Put a little differently, predictions of what AI would accomplish were
> deduced from a set of assumptions, one of which was that consciousness
> could be adequately described as mechanism. The failure of such predictions
> is then evidence against all of the assumptions--including that one...

> Does anyone see a hole in the argument?

The first hole is that the failure of a prediction usually falsifies 
specific combinations of assumptions, not individual assumptions.
For example, the falsification of "(consciousness is mechanistic) and 
(if consciousness is mechanistic AI can be implemented in this century)" 
doesn't falsify (consciousness is mechanistic).  

The second hole Perry pointed out is that falsification is an attribute
of scientific theories, not engineering proposals.  Consequently
the claims of AI were not set forth in falsifiable terms, nor 
at such an an abstract philosophical level.  Instead the claims
went along the lines of "if you give us enough money we'll have
smart robots on the battlefield by 1990".  Engineers' claims about
what they will deliver when for how much are notoriously unfalsifiable. :-)

