From: pcm@cs.brown.edu (Peter C. McCluskey)
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul?
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute freely

 I would like to suggest an analogy from a hypothetical economic experiment.
Many people, including some economists, claim that cutting tax rates helps
economic growth, and a number of these people predict that cutting tax rates
by x% will cause enough economic growth to balance the budget by year y.
Tax rates are cut by an amount which falls within the range of values
suggested for x, and the budget is not balanced by the median of the values
suggested for y. Would this discredit the theory that cutting tax rates
increases economic growth?

 When theories consist only of technical assertions made by specialists
who form a well-defined consensus about the details of the theories and
can agree on what those theories predict, then failure of those predictions
discredits the theories. But with any subject which generates a wide variety
of forecasts, a significant fraction of those forecasts will be unjustified
by the basic theory (assuming a single such theory even exists), and the
existence of falsified predictions has substantially less significance.
Also, the most colorful predictions are more likely to be remembered
better (at least by non-specialists) than the most carefully reasoned
forecasts.

